misc: hy310-motor: add a bias override to test the limiter pad

The limiter has never once read active on this unit, not at probe, not during
homing, and not while sweeping the mechanism by hand through a position where
the picture is sharp. The pad is configured exactly as stock has it --
verified on the running board:

    stock  pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down, output drive strength (20 mA)
    ours   pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down (1 ohms), drive strength (20 mA)

so the configuration is no longer the difference between us and stock. What is
left is either the mechanism sitting outside the switch's range, or the input
being electrically dead.

Those two cannot be told apart by a read: the switch sources 3.3 V when active
and is open otherwise, so an open switch and a severed wire both leave a
pull-down reading low. A pull-up does not separate them either -- but it does
answer a different question, the one that is still open: whether the input
stage works at all. An undriven, healthy pad follows a pull-up and reads high;
a pad that is shorted or damaged stays low. Since our own earlier driver drove
this line as an output against a switch that sources 3.3 V, on every boot,
damage is a real possibility and worth ruling in or out.

Add the override as a read-only module parameter, and log the raw value right
after the bias is applied so the answer is in dmesg without any further poking.
Default -1 keeps the current behaviour exactly.

    insmod hy310-focus-motor.ko limiter_bias=1   # pull-up, the diagnostic
    insmod hy310-focus-motor.ko                  # unchanged

diff -ruN a/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c b/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c
--- a/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c	2026-09-07 20:29:59.184496008 +0200
+++ b/drivers/misc/hy310-focus-motor.c	2026-09-07 20:30:23.375639048 +0200
@@ -804,6 +804,26 @@
  * For this board that is a pull-down, which is what the stock system shows on
  * PH14 (stock_pinconf_pio.txt: "pin 238 (PH14): input bias pull down").
  */
+/*
+ * Diagnostic override for the limiter pad bias. The switch on this board
+ * sources 3.3 V when it is active and is open otherwise, so an open switch and
+ * a broken connection look identical on a pull-down. A pull-up does not tell
+ * those two apart either -- but it does say whether the input stage itself is
+ * healthy: with a pull-up an undriven, working pad reads high, while a pad that
+ * is shorted or damaged stays low. That is the one thing about this pin that
+ * can still be established without a meter, and the limiter has never once read
+ * active on this unit.
+ *
+ *   -1 (default) bias as the board wants it, the complement of active_level
+ *    0            no bias at all
+ *    1            pull-up   -- the diagnostic case
+ *    2            pull-down -- this board's normal case, forced
+ */
+static int limiter_bias = -1;
+module_param(limiter_bias, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(limiter_bias,
+		 "limiter pad bias: -1 auto, 0 none, 1 pull-up, 2 pull-down");
+
 static int motor_request_limiter(struct hy310_motor *m, struct device_node *np,
 				 const char *prop, int *pin_out)
 {
@@ -836,16 +856,34 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
-	bias = PIN_CONF_PACKED(m->active_level ? PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_DOWN
-					       : PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_UP, 1);
+	switch (limiter_bias) {
+	case 0:
+		bias = PIN_CONF_PACKED(PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_DISABLE, 0);
+		break;
+	case 1:
+		bias = PIN_CONF_PACKED(PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_UP, 1);
+		break;
+	case 2:
+		bias = PIN_CONF_PACKED(PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_DOWN, 1);
+		break;
+	default:
+		bias = PIN_CONF_PACKED(m->active_level ? PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_DOWN
+						       : PIN_CONFIG_BIAS_PULL_UP, 1);
+		break;
+	}
 	ret = gpiod_set_config(desc, bias);
 	if (ret && ret != -ENOTSUPP && ret != -EOPNOTSUPP)
 		dev_warn(dev, "%s: cannot set pad bias: %d\n", prop, ret);
 
 	*pin_out = gpio;
-	dev_info(dev, "%s: gpio %d, input, bias pull-%s, active %s\n",
-		 prop, gpio, m->active_level ? "down" : "up",
-		 m->active_level ? "high" : "low");
+	dev_info(dev, "%s: gpio %d, input, bias %s, active %s, reads %d\n",
+		 prop, gpio,
+		 limiter_bias == 0 ? "none" :
+		 limiter_bias == 1 ? "pull-up (override)" :
+		 limiter_bias == 2 ? "pull-down (override)" :
+		 m->active_level ? "pull-down" : "pull-up",
+		 m->active_level ? "high" : "low",
+		 gpiod_get_raw_value(desc));
 	return 0;
 }
 
